Slide 1 — Limited Public Transportation in Cuba
"For my first slide, I looked at public transportation in Cuba, New Mexico.
I used the Rio Metro Route 8 bus schedule and MATLAB to look at the departure times.
As you can see, there is one scheduled departure at 8:19 in the morning and another at 3:30 in the afternoon.
That is a gap of 7 hours and 11 minutes between the two departures.
This can be a problem for people who don't have their own car because they have fewer options for getting around.
For example, if someone needs to go to work or has a doctor's appointment, the bus schedule might not work with the time they need to travel."
Slide 2 — Wildlife Collisions on US 550
"For my second slide, I looked at wildlife collisions on US 550, which is one of the main highways near Cuba.
According to the New Mexico Department of Transportation, there were 208 reported wildlife collisions along this section of the highway.
Looking at the graph, elk had the most reported collisions with 120, followed by deer with 82, and black bears with 5.
These collisions can be dangerous for drivers and can also cause damage to vehicles or delays on the road.
One possible solution is adding wildlife crossings and fencing to help keep animals away from traffic."
Slide 3 — When the Weather Turns, the Roads Close
"For my last slide, I looked at how weather can affect road conditions around Cuba.
Some of the roads in the surrounding area are dirt or gravel, which can make traveling difficult when it rains or snows.
During heavy rain, roads can become muddy or washed out, making them difficult or even impossible to drive on.
Residents have reported problems with roads that were worked on but still weren't passable.
This can prevent people from getting to important places like the grocery store or doctor's appointments.
Overall, these three problems show how transportation in Cuba can be difficult because of limited bus schedules, wildlife collisions, and poor road conditions."

